//? Title: Gaussian Process Regression: Conditioning, Noise and Uncertainty
//? Subtitle: Exact covariance calculations, empirical-Bayes fitting and held-out iris predictions with datamunge
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given noisy measurements of a nonlinear relationship, we want both a predicted mean
and an account of the uncertainty in an unobserved curve value or a future measurement.
Those are different targets: observation noise remains even when the underlying curve
is well determined. Gaussian process regression conditions a joint Gaussian model using
a covariance kernel. We derive the posterior and profiled marginal likelihood, solve
two-point examples by hand, and distinguish shared latent values from independent noise
at repeated query locations. A generated extrapolation sweep checks reversion to a finite
prior variance. On iris, a fit of petal length against petal width illustrates hyperparameter
selection, conditional deletion diagnostics, a separate train/test assessment and the
limits of interpreting narrow plug-in intervals as universal confidence guarantees. The
held-out RMSE is 0.403 cm; 95% new-observation intervals contain 42 of 45 test flowers,
while the latent-function intervals contain 14. Far outside the observed widths, the
model returns to a prior mean of 3.758 cm with finite latent standard deviation 1.423 cm.
)

\toc

> The method

>> A function prior and two prediction targets

A Gaussian process specifies jointly Gaussian function values at every finite collection
of inputs. Write \(f\sim\mathcal{GP}(m,k)\) and observe
\(y_i=f(x_i)+\epsilon_i\), where independent observation errors have variance
\(\sigma_n^2\). The squared-exponential or RBF kernel is

$$
 k(x,x')=s\exp\left(-\frac{\|x-x'\|^2}{2\ell^2}\right)=sR(x,x').
$$
\alttext(The covariance is signal variance s times an RBF correlation, which decays with squared distance divided by twice the squared length scale.)

Here \(\ell\) controls correlation distance and \(s\) is a variance, not a standard
deviation. An isotropic length scale uses the same distance scale in every expanded
predictor coordinate. Scaling the inputs therefore changes the model. The default
standardises each formula-expanded predictor using its training population mean and
standard deviation. A formula intercept is suppressed; the prior mean is instead the
empirical response mean. Transformations and categorical expansions affect distance
through their resulting design columns, not through the original column names alone.

The response is always represented as \(z=(y-\bar y)/s_y\), with population standard
deviation \(s_y\); a scale below \(10^{-12}\) is replaced by one. In the following
matrix formulas, \(s\) is in standardized-response variance units and
\(\eta=\sigma_n^2/s\). A dimensionless numerical ridge \(j\) gives

$$
 C=R+(\eta+j)I,\qquad K=sC=LL^\top,\qquad \alpha=K^{-1}z.
$$
\alttext(The normalized training covariance C adds the physical noise ratio eta and numerical ridge ratio j to the RBF correlation matrix. Multiplying by s gives K, which is factored by Cholesky.)

The default ridge is \(j=10^{-8}\). It is included consistently in likelihood selection
and the final factorisation. It is not added as noise on a new query target. The exact
hand calculations below set \(j=0\) on positive-definite examples. With zero noise and
zero ridge, duplicate training inputs make the covariance singular.

For query inputs, let \(K_*=sR_{X,*}\) and \(K_{**}=sR_{*,*}\). Conditioning the
joint Gaussian, equivalently completing its quadratic form, gives

$$
 \mu_* = \bar y\mathbf1+s_yK_*^\top\alpha,\qquad
 \Sigma_f=s_y^2(K_{**}-K_*^\top K^{-1}K_*),\qquad
 \Sigma_{\mathrm{new}\ y}=\Sigma_f+s_y^2s\eta I.
$$
\alttext(The posterior mean shifts the empirical prior mean by a covariance-weighted response. Latent covariance subtracts the information supplied by training observations from prior covariance. Future observations add independent noise on their covariance diagonal.)

The mean uses two triangular solves after Cholesky; covariance reduction can use
\(V=L^{-1}K_*\) and \(V^\top V\). No explicit inverse is needed for prediction.
The Schur complement is positive semidefinite in exact arithmetic. Rounding-sized
negative variances are clipped; materially invalid values cause an error.
These equations follow [Rasmussen and Williams, chapter 2|https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW2.pdf].

`predict_detail()` describes the *latent function*. `predict_observation_detail()`
describes a *new noisy observation*. `prediction_table()` puts both side by side,
and `predict_covariance()` preserves dependence between several queries. A central
pointwise interval is mean plus or minus a Gaussian quantile times the relevant
posterior standard deviation. It is not a simultaneous confidence band, nor an
interval that integrates uncertainty in fitted hyperparameters and preprocessing.

>> Profiling the amplitude and searching a bounded grid

For fixed \(\ell,\eta,j\), put \(q=z^\top C^{-1}z\). The standardized log density is

$$
 \mathcal L(s)=-\frac12\left[n\log(2\pi)+n\log s+\log|C|+\frac qs\right],\qquad
 \hat s=\max\left(10^{-12},\frac qn\right).
$$
\alttext(The Gaussian log density combines normalization, covariance determinant and a quadratic data-fit term. Differentiating with respect to s gives q over n, subject to the library's positive variance floor.)

Differentiation gives \(-n/(2s)+q/(2s^2)=0\). At the unconstrained optimum \(q/\hat s=n\),
but that simplification is invalid when the variance floor is active. In particular,
a constant response has \(z=0\): its covariance scale is driven to the imposed floor,
not to a well-identified nonzero prior variance.

This is conditional maximum likelihood with empirical centring and scaling held fixed.
It integrates out the Gaussian function values, then plugs in covariance parameters;
it does not integrate over those parameters. In that sense it is an empirical-Bayes
fit rather than a fully integrated Bayesian posterior. The normalized-scale accessor
`signal_variance()` retains the original API convention. Original response variances
multiply by \(s_y^2\); `model_summary()` names these explicitly. Original-scale log
density subtracts \(n\log s_y\), treating the estimated scale as fixed.

By default the library searches 20 geometrically spaced lengths from \(0.1\sqrt p\)
to \(3\sqrt p\), and 15 noise ratios from \(10^{-4}\) to 1. A positive length or
nonnegative noise ratio fixes that quantity; negative finite values request selection.
Every candidate profiles \(s\). The first largest finite log density wins. This is a
bounded grid search, not a continuous or global optimisation guarantee. The profile
at each length maximizes over the searched noise ratios; `hyperparameter_search()`
exposes the full grid, including failed candidates. See
[Rasmussen and Williams, chapter 5|https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW5.pdf].

At fixed \(\ell,\eta,j\), the posterior mean uses \(R_*^\top C^{-1}z\): the amplitude
cancels. Changing noise ratio and profiling the amplitude can change both smoothing
and uncertainty, so a comparison across refitted models is not a fixed-prior variance
monotonicity argument.

>> Conditional deletion is a diagnostic, not a refitted pipeline

Let \(A=K^{-1}\). Block inversion of the Gaussian training covariance gives

$$
 \mu_{-i}=\bar y+s_y\left(z_i-\frac{\alpha_i}{A_{ii}}\right),\qquad
 v_{-i,\mathrm{new}\ y}=s_y^2\left(\frac1{A_{ii}}-sj\right).
$$
\alttext(The conditional deleted-row mean removes alpha i divided by the inverse covariance diagonal from the normalized response. The physical observation variance subtracts the held-out target's numerical ridge from the usual inverse-diagonal formula.)

When \(j=0\), the variance is the familiar inverse-diagonal expression. The subtraction
for nonzero ridge excludes numerical jitter on the new held-out target while retaining
regularisation on the observations used to predict it. `training_details()` reports these
means, residuals, observation standard deviations and standardized residuals.

The historical `fitted_values()`, `rmse()` and `r_squared()` accessors use these conditional
deletion means. They hold the response mean, feature scales, amplitude and selected kernel
parameters from the *full* sample fixed. They therefore do not equal a sequence of refitted
models with each row removed, and do not provide an unbiased assessment of the complete
tuning pipeline. Full-data posterior means are exposed separately as
`posterior_fitted_values()`. A train/test split below keeps test responses out of preprocessing
and tuning. Its empirical interval coverage assesses the new-observation target.

>> Computation and limits

An exact dense fit stores \(O(n^2+np)\) numbers. A grid of \(G\) candidates costs
\(O(G(n^3+n^2p))\) work: each uses kernel construction and Cholesky. Conditional deletion
uses solves for the inverse diagonal after selection. At \(M\) queries, marginal predictions
cost \(O(M(n^2+np))\); the joint covariance additionally costs \(O(M^2(n+p))\) and stores
\(M^2\) entries. This is a small-to-moderate-data implementation, not a sparse GP approximation.

Far from all training inputs, an RBF cross-covariance vanishes. The mean returns to
\(\bar y\), latent variance approaches \(s_y^2s\), and observation variance approaches
\(s_y^2s(1+\eta)\). Uncertainty saturates at that finite, fitted prior level; it does not
grow without bound or guarantee protection against an inappropriate prior mean or kernel.

> Simple worked examples

>> Two distinct inputs: shrinkage and a two-by-two covariance

Take \(x=(0,1)\), \(y=(-1,1)\), disable predictor standardisation and choose
\(\ell=1/\sqrt{2\log2}\), \(\eta=1/2\), \(j=0\). The response already has mean zero
and population standard deviation one. Then

$$
 R=\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\1/2&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
 C=\begin{pmatrix}3/2&1/2\\1/2&3/2\end{pmatrix},\quad
 C^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}3/4&-1/4\\-1/4&3/4\end{pmatrix},\quad
 q=2,\quad \hat s=1.
$$
\alttext(The two training inputs have correlation one half. Adding noise ratio one half gives diagonal three halves. The inverse has diagonal three quarters and off-diagonal minus one quarter. The quadratic form is two, so the profiled signal variance is one.)

Here \(\alpha=(-1,1)\), so the latent posterior means at the observations are
\((-1/2,1/2)\), rather than interpolating the noisy responses. Direct multiplication gives

$$
 \Sigma_f=\frac1{16}\begin{pmatrix}5&1\\1&5\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 \Sigma_{\mathrm{new}\ y}=\frac1{16}\begin{pmatrix}13&1\\1&13\end{pmatrix}.
$$
\alttext(The latent covariance has diagonal five sixteenths and off-diagonal one sixteenth. Adding independent noise one half raises only the diagonal to thirteen sixteenths.)

At the midpoint, the posterior mean is zero and latent variance is
\(1-1/\sqrt2\). It is slightly smaller than the variance at either observed input:
posterior uncertainty depends on the entire covariance geometry, not just nearest-point distance.
The conditional deletion means are \((1/3,-1/3)\), giving RMSE \(4/3\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gpr_two_points.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame data; data.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,1}); data.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{-1,1});
  dm::stats::GaussianProcessRegressionOptions options;
  options.length_scale=1/std::sqrt(2*std::log(2.0)); options.noise_ratio=.5; options.standardize=false; options.jitter_ratio=0;
  const dm::stats::GaussianProcessRegression gp(data,"y ~ x",options);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame query; query.add_column("x",dm::seql(-2,3,201).values());
  const auto prediction=gp.prediction_table(query); const auto& x=query.numeric_column("x").values();
  auto fit=gp.plot_fit(data), uncertainty=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  fit.title("Conditioning shrinks noisy observations").y_limits(-2,4);
  uncertainty.lines(x,prediction.numeric_column("latent_sd").values(),"Latent SD",{37,99,235},3)
    .lines(x,prediction.numeric_column("observation_sd").values(),"New-y SD",{220,90,35},3)
    .title("Two different prediction targets").x_label("Query x").y_label("Posterior standard deviation").y_limits(0,1.8);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(fit).add(uncertainty);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build"); layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/gpr_two_points.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n",gp.model_summary().select({"length_scale","signal_variance_y","noise_variance_y","conditional_loo_rmse","posterior_rmse"}).to_md(6),
      gp.training_details().to_md(6));
}
```
