| 0.849322 | 1 | 0.5 | 1.333333 | 0.5 |


| row | observed | posterior_mean | loo_mean | loo_residual | loo_observation_sd | loo_z |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | -1 | -0.5 | 0.333333 | -1.333333 | 1.154701 | -1.154701 |
| 2 | 1 | 0.5 | -0.333333 | 1.333333 | 1.154701 | 1.154701 |

\image(figures/gpr_two_points.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The exact two-point model. Left bounds describe the latent curve, while the right panel separates latent and future-observation standard deviations. Training posterior and conditional deletion means are different quantities.)
\alttext(Left: the posterior mean passes through minus one half and plus one half at the two noisy training inputs, with pointwise latent bounds. Right: latent standard deviation dips near the inputs, while observation standard deviation is larger everywhere.)

>> Repeated locations: identical latent values, distinct future errors

Now put both observations at \(x=0\), keeping responses \((-1,1)\), length one,
noise ratio one half and zero ridge. The correlation matrix is all ones; its contrast
direction has variance supplied entirely by the noise ratio. Profiling gives \(s=2\),
so physical noise variance is one. The posterior mean at zero is zero and its latent
variance is \(2-8/5=2/5\).

For two new queries at this same location, the latent values refer to the *same random
function value*. Their covariance matrix is rank one. The observation errors are instead
independent, so

$$
 \Sigma_f=\begin{pmatrix}2/5&2/5\\2/5&2/5\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 \Sigma_{\mathrm{new}\ y}=\begin{pmatrix}7/5&2/5\\2/5&7/5\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 \operatorname{Var}(f_1-f_2)=0,\quad \operatorname{Var}(y_1-y_2)=2.
$$
\alttext(Repeated latent queries have covariance two fifths in every entry, so their difference has zero variance. Independent observation noise raises each diagonal by one and gives the difference variance two.)

Treating pointwise standard deviations as if they implied independent function values
would miss this distinction. Zero noise and zero ridge cannot fit these conflicting,
coincident observations: the training covariance is singular, and the library rejects it.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gpr_repeated_queries.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame data; data.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,0}); data.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{-1,1});
  dm::stats::GaussianProcessRegressionOptions options; options.length_scale=1; options.noise_ratio=.5; options.standardize=false; options.jitter_ratio=0;
  const dm::stats::GaussianProcessRegression gp(data,"y ~ x",options);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  for (bool observation : {false,true}) {
    const auto C=gp.predict_covariance(data,observation); auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.image({1,2},{1,2},{{C(0,0),C(0,1)},{C(1,0),C(1,1)}},dm::graphics::diverging_colors(15),0,1.5);
    for (std::size_t i=0;i<2;++i) for (std::size_t j=0;j<2;++j)
      plot.text(i+1,j+1,std::format("{:.1f}",C(i,j)),C(i,j)>1?dm::RGB{255,255,255}:dm::RGB{25,25,25},22);
    plot.title(observation?"New observations: independent noise":"Latent values: the same location")
      .x_label("Query row").y_label("Query row").x_limits(.5,2.5).y_limits(.5,2.5);
    layout.add(plot);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build"); layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/gpr_repeated_queries.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",gp.prediction_table(data).to_md(6));
}
```
