> A dataset example: iris petal length from petal width

>> Data and units

`dm::datasets::iris()` has 150 rows, four numeric measurements in centimetres and a
species label. Each species contributes 50 flowers. We retain the original example's
formula `Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width`. Species describes the mixture and stratifies the
later split; it is not supplied as a predictor. A one-dimensional Gaussian-noise GP
therefore approximates a pooled relationship, not separate species-specific mechanisms.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto data=dm::datasets::iris();std::print("{} flowers, {} columns.\n\n",data.nrows(),data.ncols());
  std::print("{}\n",data | dm::group_by("Species")
    | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Width","mean_width_cm"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Length","mean_length_cm"),
                    dm::agg::min("Petal.Width","min_width_cm"),dm::agg::max("Petal.Width","max_width_cm")) | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
150 flowers, 5 columns.

| Species | n | mean_width_cm | mean_length_cm | min_width_cm | max_width_cm |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 50 | 0.246 | 1.462 | 0.1 | 0.6 |
| versicolor | 50 | 1.326 | 4.260 | 1.0 | 1.8 |
| virginica | 50 | 2.026 | 5.552 | 1.4 | 2.5 |
// result_end
\caption(Species counts and measurements in centimetres. All rows are complete for the chosen formula; there is no response-based deletion or data standardisation before a split.)

>> Full-data description and the likelihood profile

First fit the descriptive model on all flowers using the default 20-by-15 search.
Its length scale is in standardized petal-width units. The summary table reports signal
and noise variances in square centimetres, and log density on the original response
scale. The profile plot uses standardized-response log density; the Jacobian shift is
constant across candidates, so it does not change which candidate is selected.

The posterior curve and latent interval describe uncertainty about the fitted mean
function. They are not a predictive envelope for flower-to-flower variability. Compare
the training posterior RMSE with the conditional deletion RMSE, then reserve the latter's
interpretation as an internal diagnostic.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gpr_iris_fit_profile.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto data=dm::datasets::iris();const dm::stats::GaussianProcessRegression gp(data,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width");
  auto fit=gp.plot_fit(data), profile=gp.plot_length_scale_profile();
  fit.title("Pooled iris: mean and latent bounds").x_label("Petal width (cm)").y_label("Petal length (cm)").y_limits(.5,9);
  profile.title("Amplitude and noise profiled on a grid");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);layout.add(fit).add(profile);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/gpr_iris_fit_profile.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n",gp.model_summary().select({"length_scale","noise_ratio","signal_variance_y","noise_variance_y","log_likelihood_y"}).to_md(6),
    gp.model_summary().select({"conditional_loo_rmse","conditional_loo_r_squared","posterior_rmse"}).to_md(6));
}
```
