| conditional_loo_rmse | conditional_loo_r_squared | posterior_rmse |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.387653 | 0.951454 | 0.374133 |

\image(figures/gpr_iris_fit_profile.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The full-data posterior mean with a 95% pointwise latent interval and the profiled length-scale likelihood. Variances in the table use original squared-centimetre units.)
\alttext(Left: iris petal length is plotted against petal width with a nonlinear posterior mean and narrow latent bounds. Right: the log marginal likelihood is profiled over the searched noise ratios and the selected length is marked.)

The full-data grid selects length 1.024854 and noise ratio 0.071969. In original units,
signal variance is 2.026148 square centimetres and noise variance is 0.145819 square
centimetres, a noise standard deviation of 0.382 cm. Conditional deletion RMSE is
0.387653 cm, versus posterior training RMSE 0.374133 cm. The fitted log density is
4.1835 on the standardized scale and -80.5628 on the original response scale; the
change is the stated Jacobian, not a different fitted kernel.

>> Hold out flowers before preprocessing and tuning

A seeded split holds out 30 percent within each species: 105 training flowers and
45 test flowers. The GP sees only training rows during formula resolution, scaling,
amplitude profiling and the entire hyperparameter search. `score(test)` assesses these
external predictions without fitting on test responses. It reports RMSE, empirical
coverage of each interval target, mean observation-interval width and Gaussian negative
log predictive density, which rewards accurate distributions rather than wide intervals alone.

The left panel below still uses the training model's conditional deletion residuals.
The right panel uses actual held-out flowers and observation prediction intervals.
Keeping these labels separate avoids presenting the conditional deletion shortcut as
validation of a fully refitted pipeline. One small, balanced holdout is an assessment
of this split, not a population-wide coverage guarantee or a comparison of all kernels.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gpr_iris_holdout.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),.3,42,"Species");
  const dm::stats::GaussianProcessRegression gp(train,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width");
  const auto prediction=gp.prediction_table(test);const auto& actual=test.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values();
  auto residuals=gp.plot_residuals_vs_fitted(), heldout=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  residuals.title("Training: conditional deletion residuals");
  heldout.segments(actual,prediction.numeric_column("observation_lower").values(),actual,prediction.numeric_column("observation_upper").values(),{170,170,170},1.5)
    .points(actual,prediction.numeric_column("mean").values(),"Mean",{37,99,235},4)
    .abline(0,1,{80,80,80}).title("Held-out flowers: new-y intervals")
    .x_label("Observed petal length (cm)").y_label("Prediction and 95% new-y interval")
    .x_limits(.5,8).y_limits(0,9);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);layout.add(residuals).add(heldout);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/gpr_iris_holdout.svg");
  std::print("{} training rows, {} held-out rows.\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",train.nrows(),test.nrows(),
    gp.model_summary().select({"length_scale","noise_ratio","conditional_loo_rmse","posterior_rmse"}).to_md(6),gp.score(test).select({"n","rmse","latent_coverage","observation_coverage","mean_observation_width","mean_nlpd"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
105 training rows, 45 held-out rows.

| length_scale | noise_ratio | conditional_loo_rmse | posterior_rmse |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1.753443 | 0.037276 | 0.386267 | 0.372383 |


| n | rmse | latent_coverage | observation_coverage | mean_observation_width | mean_nlpd |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 45 | 0.402965 | 0.311111 | 0.933333 | 1.516322 | 0.510046 |

\image(figures/gpr_iris_holdout.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The training-only model's conditional diagnostics and external predictions are evaluated separately. Grey vertical bars are 95% new-observation intervals; the diagonal is perfect agreement. Coverage uses these noisy-target intervals, not latent-function intervals.)
\alttext(Left: conditional deletion residuals on training flowers are shown around a zero reference. Right: posterior means for 45 held-out petal lengths have vertical future-observation intervals, compared with the observed-equals-predicted diagonal.)

The training-only fit selects length 1.753443 and noise ratio 0.037276. Test RMSE is
0.402965 cm, versus conditional training deletion RMSE 0.386267 cm. Observation
interval coverage is 42/45 (93.3%); latent interval coverage of test measurements is
14/45 (31.1%), reflecting their different targets. Mean observation-interval width is
1.5163 cm and mean negative log predictive density is 0.5100. These counts are
properties of this particular test split and fitted noise model.

>> Extrapolation and the constant prior mean

Return to the full-data model and query widths 0.2, 1.3 and 2.5 cm within the training
range, then 10 cm far outside it. The output includes both interval targets. Far from
observed widths, the constant empirical prior pulls the mean towards the overall petal
length mean, even though that need not describe a flower with a much wider petal.
The bounded prior variance should not be read as an automatic guarantee against such
model misspecification. RBF smoothness, Gaussian homogeneous noise and plug-in parameter
values are all substantive assumptions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gpr_iris_extrapolation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto data=dm::datasets::iris();const dm::stats::GaussianProcessRegression gp(data,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame grid;grid.add_column("Petal.Width",dm::seql(.1,10,251).values());const auto p=gp.prediction_table(grid);
  const auto& x=grid.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values();auto curve=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), uncertainty=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  curve.points(data.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values(),data.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values(),"Observed",{130,130,130,.5},3)
    .lines(x,p.numeric_column("observation_lower").values(),"New-y bounds",{220,90,35}).lines(x,p.numeric_column("observation_upper").values(),"",{220,90,35})
    .lines(x,p.numeric_column("latent_lower").values(),"Latent bounds",{150,70,180}).lines(x,p.numeric_column("latent_upper").values(),"",{150,70,180})
    .lines(x,p.numeric_column("mean").values(),"Mean",{37,99,235},3).abline_h(gp.response_mean(),{130,130,130})
    .title("Far predictions return to the prior mean").x_label("Petal width (cm)").y_label("Petal length (cm)").y_limits(0,10);
  uncertainty.lines(x,p.numeric_column("latent_sd").values(),"Latent SD",{37,99,235},3)
    .lines(x,p.numeric_column("observation_sd").values(),"New-y SD",{220,90,35},3)
    .title("Uncertainty plateaus at the fitted prior").x_label("Petal width (cm)").y_label("Posterior SD (cm)");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);layout.add(curve).add(uncertainty);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/gpr_iris_extrapolation.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame query;query.add_column("Petal.Width",std::vector<double>{.2,1.3,2.5,10});auto table=gp.prediction_table(query);
  table.add_column("width_cm",query.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values());
  std::print("{}\n",table.select({"width_cm","mean","latent_sd","observation_sd","latent_lower","latent_upper","observation_lower","observation_upper"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| width_cm | mean | latent_sd | observation_sd | latent_lower | latent_upper | observation_lower | observation_upper |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.2 | 1.413075 | 0.058087 | 0.386255 | 1.299226 | 1.526924 | 0.656029 | 2.170122 |
| 1.3 | 4.288771 | 0.058430 | 0.386307 | 4.174250 | 4.403292 | 3.531623 | 5.045919 |
| 2.5 | 5.733776 | 0.145192 | 0.408533 | 5.449205 | 6.018346 | 4.933065 | 6.534486 |
| 10.0 | 3.758000 | 1.423428 | 1.473759 | 0.968132 | 6.547868 | 0.869485 | 6.646515 |

\image(figures/gpr_iris_extrapolation.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Within-range and extrapolated predictions from the full-data descriptive model. Both bands are pointwise and conditional on the fitted parameters. The prior mean reference is the training response mean, not a physical model of extreme flowers.)
\alttext(Left: the curve fits the observed width range then returns towards the overall petal-length mean as width approaches ten centimetres. Right: latent and new-observation standard deviations rise and approach finite plateaus, with the noisy target always wider.)

At width 10 cm the mean is 3.758 cm, the latent SD is 1.423428 cm and the
new-observation SD is 1.473759 cm. Their variances differ by the fitted noise variance
0.145819 square centimetres. The finite prior limit is visible; it does not establish
that this is an appropriate prediction for such an extreme hypothetical flower.

> Conclusion

Two-point calculations identify what the matrix algebra means: conditioning shrinks
the posterior mean away from noisy observations, while independent future noise adds
to the covariance diagonal. Repeated-location queries show why a collection of marginal
standard deviations cannot replace a joint covariance matrix. The extrapolation sweep
checks both the exact noise increment and the finite prior limit.

On iris, likelihood selection supplies a reproducible, model-dependent covariance fit.
Conditional deletion is useful for diagnosis, while the held-out split assesses a model
whose preprocessing and tuning did not see test responses. Latent and observation intervals
answer different questions; neither includes hyperparameter or mean-estimation uncertainty.
More data, different kernels, heteroscedastic models or a different prior mean could change
the predictions and their calibration. A GP provides an explicit conditional probability
model, whose assumptions still need to match the prediction problem.

> References

- C. E. Rasmussen and C. K. I. Williams (2006). *Gaussian Processes for Machine Learning*. MIT Press. [Book and original chapter files|https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/]. Chapters [2: regression|https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW2.pdf] and [5: hyperparameter selection and conditional deletion|https://gaussianprocess.org/gpml/chapters/RW5.pdf].
- scikit-learn developers. [GaussianProcessRegressor|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.gaussian_process.GaussianProcessRegressor.html], used as an independent check with fixed ConstantKernel times RBF, matching normalization and a training-only diagonal alpha.
